`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["A real control system is never a single box. Think of a room heater with a thermostat: there is a controller (the thermostat's electronics), an actuator (the heating element), the room itself (which warms up slowly), and a sensor (the thermometer) that sends the measured temperature back so it can be compared with the temperature you asked for. Each of these parts has its own transfer function. To predict how the ",s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"whole"})," system behaves — how fast the room warms up, whether it overshoots, what temperature it settles at — we need ",s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"one"})," transfer function from the set-point to the room temperature."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["A ",s.jsx(b,{en:"block diagram",tr:"blok şema"})," is the picture that shows how the parts are connected, and ",s.jsx(b,{en:"block diagram reduction",tr:"blok sadeleştirme"})," is the set of techniques that collapses that picture, step by step, into a single block"]}),`
